  {
    "path": "/private/rounds/0/manual_recap",
    "old_sha256": "3ec6a7c6b27459190ca8ea0003b124edee59dc733705430c5e3f4e99789cf12e",
    "value": "Module submission_util.py, pure Python with no imports; every public function takes exactly one JSON value and returns a JSON-safe object. Available now: the working helper normalize_submission(payload), which validates one submission object — non-empty student and assignment strings returned trimmed, and a numeric score from 0 to 100 inclusive with booleans rejected — answering {\"ok\": true, \"record\": {\"student\", \"assignment\", \"score\"}} or {\"ok\": false, \"error\": message} with the first applicable message, and never raising. This round adds validate_batch(batch): a non-list batch returns exactly {\"ok\": false, \"error\": \"batch must be a list\"}; otherwise every element is normalized in arrival order and the result is {\"ok\": true, \"valid\": [...], \"errors\": [...], \"counts\": {\"total\", \"valid\", \"invalid\"}}, where errors are {\"index\", \"error\"} pairs in arrival order carrying the helper's messages, and an all-invalid batch still returns \"ok\": true. That index-and-message error reporting is this feature's own contract from its request; no later feature has been told yet to return such a list. Standing rules in force: each function takes one JSON value and must always return a plain JSON-safe object, with bad input producing an error object rather than an exception; never sort or reorder output — the assistant's alphabetical-sorting suggestion was explicitly declined, so sorting is prohibited, not just optional; attempts are never deduplicated or merged, resubmissions included; and any average reported by a future feature is, for now, rounded to two decimals."
  },
