  {
    "path": "/private/rounds/2/manual_recap",
    "old_sha256": "406c552ffa6b3e4ddaf8f01685ee056ef65b7d70d9e1645a1c73205869a70373",
    "value": "validate_batch and group_submissions are delivered and settled; new work builds on group_submissions rather than revalidating. This round adds summarize_class(batch): a non-list batch passes the underlying error object through unchanged; otherwise the result is {\"ok\": true, \"assignments\": {name: {\"submissions\", \"students\", \"average\"}}, \"overall\": {\"submissions\", \"students\", \"average\"}, \"errors\": [...]}. For the summary, errors is carried over from grouping unchanged and is always present, even when empty — the assistant's suggestion to drop it was explicitly rejected; that always-present-errors requirement is the summary's own contract from m11 and m12. Summary-specific counting, scoped to this feature only: within an assignment, a student who resubmitted counts exactly once, with their best score — submissions is the number of counted scores, students the number of distinct students, average the mean of the counted scores rounded to one decimal. overall pools every counted score across all assignments for submissions and average, counts students distinct across the whole class rather than summing per-assignment student counts, and reports average null when nothing was counted at all. Validation and grouping still keep every attempt; the best-score counting applies only inside this summary and does not authorize deduplication in validation or grouping. One-decimal rounding is the current convention and now has its first consumer; the retired two-decimal convention must not be used. All other standing rules persist: JSON in, JSON-safe object out, never raise, and arrival order preserved wherever record order is observable."
  },
